# Enable a Cross-Chain Token
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/use-cases/enable-cross-chain-token

This use case explains **when and why a token issuer would enable cross-chain transfers** using CCIP.

**Intent:** Token-based

Before building, check the [CCIP Directory](/ccip/directory/mainnet) to confirm supported networks, tokens, and cross-chain lanes for your use case.

***

## What you can do

Enable a token to move securely across blockchains while maintaining a **single canonical supply** and **issuer-defined controls**.

With CCIP, you can:

- Make a token natively usable across multiple blockchains
- Preserve supply integrity across chains
- Configure mint/burn or lock/mint behavior per network
- Apply rate limits, permissions, and administrative controls
- Rely on CCIP’s cross-chain security and execution guarantees

***

## Who this is for

This use case is designed for **asset issuers and protocol owners**, including:

- Stablecoin, RWA, and tokenized asset issuers
- DeFi protocols issuing governance or utility tokens
- Financial institutions and enterprises distributing assets across chains

***

## Problem this solves

Issuing the same asset on multiple blockchains typically leads to:

- Fragmented liquidity
- Wrapped or bridged assets with inconsistent guarantees
- Loss of issuer control
- Operational complexity and reconciliation risk

CCIP solves this by enabling **cross-chain tokens** that behave as *one asset across many chains*, without relying on ad-hoc bridges or wrappers.

***

## When to use this

Use this approach if:

- You issue or control the token contract
- The token needs to move across chains
- Supply integrity and issuer controls matter
- The asset will be consumed by third-party applications

***

## When *not* to use this

This may be unnecessary if:

- You only need occasional value transfers
- You do not control the token contract
- A simple application-level transfer is sufficient

In those cases, see [Build a cross-chain app](/ccip/use-cases/build-cross-chain-app) instead.

## How it works (high level)

CCIP-enabled tokens use standardized token pools and transfer logic to move value between chains. Each transfer is verified by decentralized oracle networks and enforced onchain.

This model allows tokens to move across chains without fragmenting liquidity or introducing external bridge trust assumptions.

## Everything you need to enable a Cross-Chain Token

Use this section to **orient yourself** and understand which resources exist.\
You do not need to consume everything here to get started.

### Understand the model

- **[Cross-Chain Tokens (CCTs)](/ccip/concepts/cross-chain-token)**\
  How cross-chain tokens behave, how supply is preserved, and which token handling mechanisms exist.

- **[Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/concepts/core-concepts)**\
  Shared primitives such as routers, token pools, and execution guarantees.

### Follow the tutorials

