# Build a Cross-Chain App
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/use-cases/build-cross-chain-app

This use case describes **application-level patterns for building cross-chain dApps** with CCIP.

**Intent:** Token-based and data-driven (combined)

Most cross-chain applications are implemented using one or more of the following:

- [Programmable Token Transfers (PTT)](/ccip/tutorials/evm/programmable-token-transfers)
- [Cross-Chain Messaging](/ccip/tutorials/evm/send-arbitrary-data)
- [Token Transfers](/ccip/tutorials/evm/transfer-tokens-from-contract)

> Already familiar with CCIP and want to start from real code instead of step-by-step tutorials?\
> See **[Build with CCIP Starter Kits](/ccip/getting-started/build-with-starter-kits)**.

## What you can do

Build applications that communicate across blockchains by sending messages, tokens, or both, with programmable logic executed on the destination chain.

With CCIP, your application can:

- Send arbitrary messages between blockchains
- Transfer tokens as part of an application workflow
- Trigger smart contract logic on a destination chain
- Coordinate state and actions across multiple chains
- Verify delivery and execution status deterministically

Common examples of cross-chain applications include:

- **Cross-chain deposits**, where tokens are transferred to another chain and immediately credited or locked
- **Cross-chain staking**, where tokens deposited on one chain trigger staking logic on another
- **Cross-chain swaps**, where token transfers and execution are coordinated across chains
- **Governance execution**, where decisions made on one chain trigger actions on another

### Who this is for

This use case is designed for **application developers**, including:

- DeFi protocols and onchain applications
- Institutional applications and internal systems
- Middleware, orchestration, and automation layers
- Wallets and developer tooling providers

### Problem this solves

Cross-chain applications often need to:

- Accept assets or signals from multiple chains
- Trigger actions when events occur on another blockchain
- Coordinate multi-step or bidirectional workflows
- Avoid maintaining custom integrations per bridge or network

Building this reliably across chains is complex and error-prone.\
Custom bridges, ad-hoc messaging, and chain-specific integrations increase operational risk and slow development.

### How CCIP helps

CCIP provides a standardized cross-chain messaging layer that enables applications to:

- Send and receive messages and tokens across supported chains
- Attach structured data to transfers
- Rely on deterministic execution guarantees
- Track message delivery and execution status

This allows applications to focus on **business logic**, not interoperability plumbing.

### When to use this

Use this approach if:

- You are building an application that spans multiple blockchains
- Your app needs to react to events or state changes on another chain
- You consume existing cross-chain tokens but do not issue them
- You need reliable delivery, execution, and status tracking

### How this differs from **Enable a cross-chain token**

- This use case is **consumer-side**, not issuer-side
- No token supply control or pool configuration is required
- Optimized for application logic and workflows
- Tokens are inputs to your app, not assets you manage

If you control the token and need to enable it across chains, see **Enable a cross-chain token** instead.

### How it works (high level)

Your source contract sends a CCIP message that may include:

- Arbitrary encoded data
- Token transfers
- Or both together

On the destination chain, a receiver contract:

- Verifies the message via CCIP
- Executes application logic based on the payload
- Optionally emits acknowledgements or follow-up messages

CCIP handles message delivery, ordering, verification, and execution guarantees.

## Common patterns

- Cross-chain acknowledgements (A → B → A)
- Event-driven workflows across chains
- Multi-chain state synchronization
- Triggering execution with tokens + data
- Automated settlement and reconciliation

## Execution paths

### Do it now

If you want to see CCIP working end to end as quickly as possible, use the quickstart that sends a message between two chains and executes logic on the destination.

- [Quickstart - Send Your First Cross-Chain Message](/ccip/getting-started/evm)

### Go deeper

Use these tutorials to build real cross-chain application workflows.

#### Transfer tokens (recommended starting point)

- [Transfer Tokens](/ccip/tutorials/evm/transfer-tokens-from-contract)
- [Transfer Tokens with Data](/ccip/tutorials/evm/programmable-token-transfers)
- [Transfer Tokens between EOAs](/ccip/tutorials/evm/offchain/transfer-tokens-from-eoa)
- [Transfer USDC with Data](/ccip/tutorials/evm/usdc)

#### Send cross-chain messages

- [Send Arbitrary Data](/ccip/tutorials/evm/send-arbitrary-data)
- [A → B → A Confirmation Pattern](/ccip/tutorials/evm/send-arbitrary-data-receipt-acknowledgment)

#### Observe and verify execution

- [Check CCIP Message Status](/ccip/tutorials/evm/offchain/get-status-offchain)

