# Send Tokens and Data Cross-Chain
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/use-cases/send-token-data-cross-chain

This use case describes **cross-chain application patterns that combine token transfers with data**, such as deposits, staking, swaps, and multi-chain workflows.

**Intent:** Token-based and data-driven (Programmable Token Transfers)

Before building, check the [CCIP Directory](/ccip/directory/mainnet) to confirm supported networks, tokens, and cross-chain lanes for your use case.

Most production CCIP integrations today involve **token transfers**, often combined with application logic using **Programmable Token Transfers (PTT)**.

> Advanced builders who prefer starting from production-ready code may want to begin with the\
> **[Programmable Token Transfers Starter Kit](/ccip/getting-started/build-with-starter-kits)**.

## What you can do

Send tokens alongside structured data to trigger application logic, coordinate state, or execute workflows on a destination blockchain.

This enables advanced cross-chain patterns where **value transfer and instructions arrive together**, allowing applications to react deterministically on another chain.

With CCIP, you can:

- Transfer tokens with accompanying data in a single cross-chain message
- Trigger smart contract execution on the destination chain
- Coordinate multi-step, multi-chain workflows
- Execute logic conditionally based on transferred value
- Verify delivery and execution status reliably

## Who this is for

This use case is designed for **advanced application developers and protocol teams**, including:

- DeFi protocols coordinating multi-chain actions
- Applications that require atomic value + instruction delivery
- Orchestration, automation, and middleware systems
- Teams building complex cross-chain workflows

## Problem this solves

Many cross-chain applications need to:

- Transfer value and trigger logic at the same time
- Ensure instructions are executed only if value arrives
- Coordinate state across multiple chains reliably
- Avoid race conditions between messaging and transfers

Handling tokens and messages separately introduces complexity, failure modes, and synchronization risk.

## How CCIP helps

CCIP allows tokens and data to be sent together in a **single, atomic cross-chain message**, providing:

- Deterministic delivery and execution semantics
- Guaranteed ordering between value and instructions
- Unified verification and status tracking
- Reduced complexity compared to parallel message flows

This enables developers to build **workflows**, not just transfers.

## When to use this

Use this approach if:

- Your application requires tokens and instructions to arrive together
- Execution on the destination chain depends on transferred value
- You are coordinating multi-step or bidirectional workflows
- Simple messaging or token transfers alone are insufficient

## How it works (high level)

A single CCIP message includes:

- One or more token transfers
- An encoded data payload

On the destination chain, the receiver contract:

- Verifies the message via CCIP
- Receives tokens and data atomically
- Executes logic based on the payload contents

This guarantees that value and instructions remain synchronized throughout the workflow.

## Common patterns

- Paying for execution on a destination chain
- Triggering logic with contextual parameters
- Multi-chain settlement and reconciliation
- Coordinated governance or treasury actions
- A → B → A confirmation flows with value transfer

## Everything you need to build token + data workflows

Use this section to **understand the landscape** of concepts and tutorials related to programmable token transfers.

### Understand the model

- **[Programmable Token Transfers (PTT)](/ccip/tutorials/evm/programmable-token-transfers)**\
  Core patterns for combining tokens and data in a single CCIP message.

- **[Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/concepts/core-concepts)**\
  Messages, execution guarantees, ordering, and verification.

### Follow the tutorials

- **[Transfer Tokens with Data](/ccip/tutorials/evm/programmable-token-transfers)**
- **[Defensive Transfers](/ccip/tutorials/evm/programmable-token-transfers-defensive)**
- **[Transfer USDC with Data](/ccip/tutorials/evm/usdc)**

### Multi-step workflows

- **[A → B → A Confirmation Pattern](/ccip/tutorials/evm/send-arbitrary-data-receipt-acknowledgment)**

### Observe and operate

- **[Check CCIP Message Status](/ccip/tutorials/evm/offchain/get-status-offchain)**
- **[Manual Execution](/ccip/concepts/manual-execution)**

## Execution paths

