# Build with CCIP Starter Kits
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/evm/getting-started/build-with-starter-kits

This page shows how **experienced builders** can use CCIP starter kits to jump straight into production-oriented development.

Starter kits are designed for developers who prefer starting from real, maintained code rather than step-by-step tutorials.

If you’re new to CCIP, start with the [Quickstart](../getting-started) instead.

## Who this is for

This path is intended for developers who:

- Are comfortable with Hardhat or Foundry
- Want a working CCIP setup immediately
- Prefer learning by reading and extending real code
- Plan to customize or productionize cross-chain workflows

You do not need to follow the full tutorial sequence to use starter kits.

## What CCIP starter kits provide

CCIP starter kits are maintained repositories that include:

- Pre-configured CCIP contracts
- Network and lane configuration
- End-to-end examples (messaging, tokens, and token + data workflows)
- Production-safe patterns and defaults
- Clear extension points for customization

They assume familiarity with CCIP concepts and optimize for speed.

## Example workflow (high level)

A typical starter-kit workflow looks like this:

1. Clone the relevant CCIP starter kit repository
2. Configure source and destination networks
3. Run an included cross-chain example
4. Extend the sender or receiver logic for your application

This mirrors how most production CCIP integrations are built.

For full implementation details, follow the README in the starter kit repository.

## Available CCIP starter kits

Choose the starter kit that matches your development environment.

### CCIP Starter Kit — Foundry

Use this kit if you prefer **Foundry** for development and testing.

- Foundry-based project structure
- CCIP messaging and token transfer examples
- Production-oriented configuration and patterns

👉 **Repository:**\
[https://github.com/smartcontractkit/ccip-starter-kit-foundry](https://github.com/smartcontractkit/ccip-starter-kit-foundry)

### CCIP Starter Kit — Hardhat

Use this kit if you prefer **Hardhat** or a JavaScript/TypeScript-based workflow.

- Hardhat-based project structure
- CCIP messaging and token transfer examples
- Suitable for frontend-integrated or scripting-heavy setups

👉 **Repository:**\
[https://github.com/smartcontractkit/ccip-starter-kit-hardhat](https://github.com/smartcontractkit/ccip-starter-kit-hardhat)

## Where to go next

- Review **[Concepts](../concepts)** if you need to understand CCIP guarantees, architecture, or execution behavior
- Use **[Tutorials](../tutorials)** to explore specific patterns or APIs referenced in the starter kits
- Continue with the linked repositories above to extend and productionize your implementation

