# Enable your tokens in CCIP (Burn & Mint): Register from an EOA using Foundry
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/evm/tutorials/cross-chain-tokens/register-from-eoa-burn-mint-foundry
Last Updated: 2025-10-30

<PageTabs
  pages={[
  {
    name: "Hardhat (Burn & Mint)",
    url: "/ccip/evm/tutorials/cross-chain-tokens/register-from-eoa-burn-mint-hardhat",
    icon: "/images/tutorial-icons/hardhat-icn.png",
  },
  {
    name: "Foundry (Burn & Mint)",
    url: "/ccip/evm/tutorials/cross-chain-tokens/register-from-eoa-burn-mint-foundry",
    icon: "/images/tutorial-icons/foundry-icn.png",
  },
]}
/>

In this tutorial you will:

1. Deploy a token on two chains using [Foundry](https://book.getfoundry.sh/).
2. Set up a `Burn & Mint` token pool on each chain.
3. Upgrade the token to be cross-chain using the CCIP protocol.
4. Test the cross-chain token transfer by burning tokens on the source chain and minting them on the destination chain.

## Before You Begin

<Accordion title="How to configure deployment parameters?" number={2} startOpen={false}>
  Deployment parameters can be configured in two ways:

  1. Edit the `.json` files in `script/input/`.
  2. Pass environment variables inline at runtime.

  > **Note:** Environment variables take precedence over `.json` file values.

  ### Token Deployment Configuration

  Default values live in `script/input/token.json`:

  ```json filename="script/input/token.json"
  {
    "name": "BnM Test",
    "symbol": "BnM-T",
    "decimals": 18,
    "maxSupply": 0,
    "preMint": 0,
    "tokenAmountToMint": 1000000000000000000000,
    "tokenAmountToTransfer": 1000000000000000000
  }
  ```

  Deployment fields can be overridden with inline env vars:

  | Env var            | Overrides (`token.json`) | Description                                   |
  | ------------------ | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |
  | `TOKEN_NAME`       | `.name`                  | Token name                                    |
  | `TOKEN_SYMBOL`     | `.symbol`                | Token symbol                                  |
  | `TOKEN_DECIMALS`   | `.decimals`              | Decimal places (usually `18`)                 |
  | `TOKEN_MAX_SUPPLY` | `.maxSupply`             | Max supply in smallest unit (`0` = unlimited) |
  | `TOKEN_PRE_MINT`   | `.preMint`               | Amount pre-minted to deployer (`0` = none)    |

  `tokenAmountToMint` and `tokenAmountToTransfer` are not deployment parameters \
  they are used as defaults by the [Mint Tokens](#mint-tokens) and [Transfer Tokens](#transfer-tokens-across-networks) steps respectively, and can be overridden with the `AMOUNT` inline variable at runtime.

  ### Advanced Pool Hooks Configuration

  <Aside type="note" title="What are advanced pool hooks?">
    Advanced Pool Hooks are an optional security layer you can attach to a token pool. They run checks :

    <ol>
      <li>**before** tokens are locked/burned (source chain)</li>
      <li>**before** tokens are released/minted (destination chain)</li>
    </ol>
  </Aside>

  Default values live in `script/input/advanced-pool-hooks.json`:

  ```json filename="script/input/advanced-pool-hooks.json"
  {
    "allowlist": [],
    "thresholdAmount": 0,
    "policyEngine": "0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000",
    "authorizedCallers": []
  }
  ```

  All fields can be overridden with inline env vars:

  | Env var              | Overrides (`advanced-pool-hooks.json`) | Description                                                                |
  | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | `ALLOWLIST`          | `.allowlist`                           | Addresses allowed to transfer (CSV or JSON array)                          |
  | `THRESHOLD_AMOUNT`   | `.thresholdAmount`                     | Transfer amount threshold before additional CCVs are required (`0` = none) |
  | `POLICY_ENGINE`      | `.policyEngine`                        | Policy engine contract address                                             |
  | `AUTHORIZED_CALLERS` | `.authorizedCallers`                   | Addresses authorized to call the hooks (CSV or JSON array)                 |
</Accordion>

## Tutorial

<Aside type="tip" title="Already have tokens deployed?">
  1. If you already have ERC-20 tokens deployed on both chains, you can skip the `Deploy Tokens` section and start directly from
     the `Deploy Token Pools` section.
  2. Export your existing token addresses so subsequent scripts can find them:

  ```bash
  export ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_TOKEN=<your_token_address_on_sepolia>
  export AVALANCHE_FUJI_TOKEN=<your_token_address_on_fuji>
  ```

  3. Your tokens must support burn and mint operations (this tutorial deploys Burn & Mint pools). They must also
     implement either getCCIPAdmin() or OpenZeppelin's owner() so the `Claim Admin Role` step can identify the
     token administrator.
</Aside>

<Accordion title="Deploy Tokens" number={1} startOpen={false}>
  Use the `DeployToken.s.sol` script to deploy your tokens on two testnets, Ethereum Sepolia and Avalanche Fuji.

  > **Note:** The script reads the `script/input/token.json` file to get the token name, symbol, decimals, maximum supply, and pre-mint amount. \
  > You can also override these values by providing inline environment variables at runtime. \
  > See [how to configure deployment parameters](/ccip/evm/tutorials/cross-chain-tokens/register-from-eoa-burn-mint-foundry#token-deployment-configuration) for more details.

  <GitHubCard title="DeployToken.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-cct-foundry/blob/main/script/deploy/DeployToken.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    View the deployment script on GitHub.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Deploy token on Ethereum Sepolia:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
     ROLES_RECIPIENT=<your_roles_recipient_address> \
       forge script \
       script/deploy/DeployToken.s.sol \
       --rpc-url $ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL \
       --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
       --broadcast \
       --verify
     ```

     <Aside type="note" title="Verification note">
       <ol>
         <li>
           <code>--verify</code> requires <code>ETHERSCAN_API_KEY</code> to be set.
         </li>

         <li>
           <code>ROLES_RECIPIENT</code> is an optional environment variable. If not provided, the script will use the
           deployer's address as the roles recipient.
         </li>
       </ol>
     </Aside>

     Example output:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------

      [Step 1] Deploying BnM Test (BnM-T) on Ethereum Sepolia
        Token deployed at: 0x7524BBB9B6ba7D14f61A10b89818F6BB862D5491
        https://sepolia.etherscan.io/address/0x7524BBB9B6ba7D14f61A10b89818F6BB862D5491

      [Step 2] Granting mint and burn roles to: 0x3A34637a41aB08519d30Fdb65344aBa8E9b2e994
        ✅ Roles granted successfully!

        ========================================
        ✅ Deployment Complete on Ethereum Sepolia!
        ========================================
        Token Address: 0x7524BBB9B6ba7D14f61A10b89818F6BB862D5491
        https://sepolia.etherscan.io/address/0x7524BBB9B6ba7D14f61A10b89818F6BB862D5491

        Run this command to set the environment variable:
        export ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_TOKEN=0x7524BBB9B6ba7D14f61A10b89818F6BB862D5491
        ========================================

      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
     ```

     After deployment, the token address is automatically saved to:

     ```bash
     script/deployments/tokens/{CHAIN_NAME_IDENTIFIER}/{timestamp}-{SYMBOL}-Token.json
     ```

     1. The file uses the env var name as the key (for example, `ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_TOKEN`).
     2. You can copy the key and value directly into an `export` command.
     3. The `script/deployments/` directory is ignored by `.gitignore`, so these files stay local to each user.

  2. Deploy token on Avalanche Fuji:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
     ROLES_RECIPIENT=<your_roles_recipient_address> \
       forge script \
       script/deploy/DeployToken.s.sol \
       --rpc-url $AVALANCHE_FUJI_RPC_URL \
       --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
       --broadcast \
       --verify
     ```

     Example output:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
      [Step 1] Deploying BnM Test (BnM-T) on Avalanche Fuji
        Token deployed at: 0x82370f2bDE0150fA320B355a189fb475348AaCb4
        https://testnet.snowscan.xyz/address/0x82370f2bDE0150fA320B355a189fb475348AaCb4

      [Step 2] Granting mint and burn roles to: 0x3A34637a41aB08519d30Fdb65344aBa8E9b2e994
        ✅ Roles granted successfully!

        ========================================
        ✅ Deployment Complete on Avalanche Fuji!
        ========================================
        Token Address: 0x82370f2bDE0150fA320B355a189fb475348AaCb4
        https://testnet.snowscan.xyz/address/0x82370f2bDE0150fA320B355a189fb475348AaCb4

        Run this command to set the environment variable:
        export AVALANCHE_FUJI_TOKEN=0x82370f2bDE0150fA320B355a189fb475348AaCb4
        ========================================
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
     ```

     After deployment, the token address is automatically saved to:

     ```bash
     script/deployments/tokens/{CHAIN_NAME_IDENTIFIER}/{timestamp}-{SYMBOL}-Token.json
     ```

  3. Set the environment variables for the deployed token addresses:

     <Aside type="note" title="Why do I need to export these?">
       1. Deployment addresses are persisted locally under <code>script/deployments/</code>, but each script still reads
          values from your current shell environment. 2. Run the <code>export</code> commands so subsequent scripts in this
          terminal session can discover the correct addresses.
     </Aside>

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      export ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_TOKEN=0x...
      export AVALANCHE_FUJI_TOKEN=0x...
     ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Claim Admin Role" number={3} startOpen={false}>
  Use the `ClaimAdmin.s.sol` script to register your EOA as the administrator for the deployed tokens on both chains. \
  This process involves interacting with the `RegistryModuleOwnerCustom` contract to set up your EOA as the admin.

  <Aside type="note" title="Supported admin patterns">
    The script auto-detects how your token exposes its admin and uses the appropriate registration method:

    <ol>
      <li>
        If your token implements <code>getCCIPAdmin()</code>, the script calls <code>registerAdminViaGetCCIPAdmin()</code>
        .
      </li>

      <li>
        If your token uses OpenZeppelin's <code>Ownable</code> pattern (<code>owner()</code>), the script calls{" "}
        <code>registerAdminViaOwner()</code> instead.
      </li>
    </ol>

    No extra configuration is needed — both patterns are fully supported.
  </Aside>

  <GitHubCard title="ClaimAdmin.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-cct-foundry/blob/main/script/setup/ClaimAdmin.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    View the admin claim script on GitHub.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Claim the Admin role for the token on Ethereum Sepolia:

     <Aside type="note" title="Note">
       If you don't set `CCIP_ADMIN_ADDRESS` as an environment variable, the script defaults to the address of the account
       broadcasting the transaction.
     </Aside>

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
     CCIP_ADMIN_ADDRESS=<your_ccip_admin_address> \
       forge script \
       script/setup/ClaimAdmin.s.sol \
       --rpc-url $ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL \
       --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
       --broadcast
     ```

     Example output:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
      [Step 1] Claiming admin for token via getCCIPAdmin() on Ethereum Sepolia
        ✅ Admin claimed successfully!

        ========================================
        ✅ Admin Claim Complete on Ethereum Sepolia!
        ========================================
        Token Address: 0xDd13Ba083c2cAaB52eF7217EE11a90a746700CBa
        https://sepolia.etherscan.io/address/0xDd13Ba083c2cAaB52eF7217EE11a90a746700CBa
        Admin Address: 0x3A34637a41aB08519d30Fdb65344aBa8E9b2e994
        ========================================

        -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
     ```

  2. Claim the Admin role for the token on Avalanche Fuji:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
     CCIP_ADMIN_ADDRESS=<your_ccip_admin_address> \
       forge script \
       script/setup/ClaimAdmin.s.sol \
       --rpc-url $AVALANCHE_FUJI_RPC_URL \
       --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
       --broadcast
     ```

     Example output:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
      [Step 1] Claiming admin for token via getCCIPAdmin() on Avalanche Fuji
        ✅ Admin claimed successfully!

        ========================================
        ✅ Admin Claim Complete on Avalanche Fuji!
        ========================================
        Token Address: 0x72b2e4258A56Ee8528Fb5B303c819E9D43044dcF
        https://testnet.snowscan.xyz/address/0x72b2e4258A56Ee8528Fb5B303c819E9D43044dcF
        Admin Address: 0x3A34637a41aB08519d30Fdb65344aBa8E9b2e994
        ========================================

        -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
     ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Configure Token Pools" number={5} startOpen={false}>
  Use the `ApplyChainUpdates.s.sol` script to initialize the token pool configuration on each blockchain to enable cross-chain transfers between the two chains. \
  This involves linking the token pool on one blockchain to its corresponding pool on the other blockchain, allowing cross-chain token transfers.\
  You will interact with the `TokenPool` contract by calling the `applyChainUpdates()` function to set up cross-chain parameters and rate limits.

  <GitHubCard title="ApplyChainUpdates.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-cct-foundry/blob/main/script/setup/ApplyChainUpdates.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    View the pool configuration script on GitHub.
  </GitHubCard>

  <Aside type="caution" title="Rate Limiting">
    Rate limiting is disabled by default (`isEnabled: false`). With rate limiting disabled, there are **no restrictions**
    on the volume of tokens that can be transferred through the pool in any direction. To protect against large,
    unexpected outflows, set `isEnabled` to `true` and configure `capacity` (maximum bucket size) and `rate` (tokens
    refilled per second). See the [Update Rate
    Limiters](/ccip/evm/tutorials/cross-chain-tokens/update-rate-limiters-foundry) tutorial for more details.
  </Aside>

  To configure rate limits inline while applying chain updates:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
   # Configure Ethereum Sepolia → Avalanche Fuji with rate limits
  DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI \
    OUTBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_CAPACITY=1000000000000000000000 \
    OUTBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_RATE=100000000000000000 \
    INBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_CAPACITY=1000000000000000000000 \
    INBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_RATE=100000000000000000 \
    forge script \
    script/setup/ApplyChainUpdates.s.sol \
    --rpc-url $ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL \
    --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
    --broadcast
  ```

  > **Note:** You can also skip setting the rate limits for now, and run a simpler command to apply chain updates from the snippets below

  | Env var                        | Required | Description                                                                                        |
  | ------------------------------ | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | `OUTBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_CAPACITY` | No       | Token bucket capacity for outbound transfers                                                       |
  | `OUTBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_RATE`     | No       | Token bucket refill rate (tokens/second) for outbound transfers                                    |
  | `OUTBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED`  | No       | Override `isEnabled` explicitly (`true`/`false`; defaults to `true` when CAPACITY or RATE are set) |
  | `INBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_CAPACITY`  | No       | Token bucket capacity for inbound transfers                                                        |
  | `INBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_RATE`      | No       | Token bucket refill rate (tokens/second) for inbound transfers                                     |
  | `INBOUND_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED`   | No       | Override `isEnabled` explicitly (`true`/`false`; defaults to `true` when CAPACITY or RATE are set) |

  1. Configure the token pool on Ethereum Sepolia:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      # Configure Ethereum Sepolia → Avalanche Fuji
      DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI \
        forge script \
        script/setup/ApplyChainUpdates.s.sol \
        --rpc-url $ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL \
        --account $KEYSTORE_NAME \
        --broadcast
     ```

     Example output:

     ```bash filename="Terminal"
      -----------------OTHER LOGS-----------------
