# Transfer Tokens with Data
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/evm/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers
Last Updated: 2025-05-19

In this tutorial, we will use CCIP to send some `tokens + arbitrary data` from one chain to another in a single transaction. \
We will send `CCIP-BnM` tokens and a `string` data from *Ethereum Sepolia* to *Avalanche Fuji* twice:

1. Once, using `LINK` tokens.
2. And again, but using native gas tokens `ETH`.

## Before you begin

1. You should understand how to write, compile, deploy, and fund a smart contract. Go through this [tutorial](/quickstarts/deploy-your-first-contract) to get started.
2. Your account must have some `ETH` and `LINK` tokens on *Ethereum Sepolia* and `AVAX` tokens on *Avalanche Fuji*. \
   Learn how to [Acquire testnet LINK](/resources/acquire-link).
3. Check the [CCIP Directory](/ccip/directory) if you want to configure a different set of source and destination chains/tokens.
4. [Acquire CCIP test tokens](/ccip/test-tokens#evm-chains). You should have `CCIP-BnM` tokens, and `CCIP-BnM` should appear in the list of your tokens in MetaMask.

## Examine the code

<Accordion title="Build transaction payload" number={2} startOpen={false}>
  `_sendCCIPMessage` calls the `_buildCCIPMessage` helper to build a CCIP message payload using `EVM2AnyMessage` [struct](/ccip/evm/api-reference/v2.0.0/client#evm2anymessage). \
  This payload is then passed to the router's `getFee` and `ccipSend` functions.

  The payload includes:

  - `receiver`: ABI-encoded destination address (`abi.encode(_receiver)`).
  - `data`: ABI-encoded text payload (`abi.encode(_text)`).
  - `tokenAmounts`: A 1-element array containing the token address and amount to transfer.
  - `extraArgs`: Message execution parameters (for example, receiver callback `gasLimit` and `blockConfirmations`).
  - `feeToken`: The token used to pay CCIP fees (`_feeTokenAddress`). Pass the LINK token address to pay in LINK, or `address(0)` to pay in native gas.

  <Aside type="note" title="Try Tweaking the ExtraArgs">
    CCIP 2.0 expands on the customization available through extra args (`ExtraArgsV3`). When calling `sendMessage`, pass
    your desired `gasLimit` and `blockConfirmations` values: - Set `gasLimit` to suit the receiver contract's complexity
    (for example, increase it if your receiver performs heavy computation). - Set `blockConfirmations` to match your
    security requirements (`0` uses default finality).
  </Aside>

  ```solidity filename="_buildCCIPMessage"
    function _buildCCIPMessage(
      address _receiver,
      string memory _text,
      address _token,
      uint256 _amount,
      address _feeTokenAddress,
      uint32 _gasLimit,
      uint16 _blockConfirmations
    ) private pure returns (Client.EVM2AnyMessage memory) {
      Client.EVMTokenAmount[] memory tokenAmounts = new Client.EVMTokenAmount[](1);
      tokenAmounts[0] = Client.EVMTokenAmount({token: _token, amount: _amount});

      return Client.EVM2AnyMessage({
        receiver: abi.encode(_receiver),
        data: abi.encode(_text),
        tokenAmounts: tokenAmounts,
        // CCIP 2.0 (ExtraArgsV3): minimal encoding (gasLimit + blockConfirmations).
        extraArgs: ExtraArgsCodec._getBasicEncodedExtraArgsV3(_gasLimit, _blockConfirmations),
        feeToken: _feeTokenAddress
      });
    }
  ```

  {" "}

  <Aside type="caution" title="Best Practices">
    This example is simplified for educational purposes. For production code, please adhere to the following best practices:

    - **Do Not Hardcode `extraArgs`**: In this example, `extraArgs` are hardcoded within the contract for simplicity. It is recommended to make `extraArgs` mutable. For instance, you can construct `extraArgs` off-chain and pass them into your function calls, or store them in a storage variable that can be updated as needed. This approach ensures that `extraArgs` remain backward compatible with future CCIP upgrades. Refer to the [Best Practices](/ccip/evm/concepts/best-practices) guide for more information.

    - **Validate the Destination Chain**: Always ensure that the destination chain is valid and supported before sending messages.

    - **Understand `allowOutOfOrderExecution` Usage**: This example sets `allowOutOfOrderExecution` to `true` (see [GenericExtraArgsV2](/ccip/v1/evm/api-reference/v1.6.1/client#genericextraargsv2)). Read the [Best Practices: Setting `allowOutOfOrderExecution`](/ccip/evm/concepts/best-practices#setting-allowoutoforderexecution) to learn more about this parameter.

    - **Understand CCIP Service Limits**: Review the [CCIP Service Limits](/ccip/service-limits) for constraints on message data size, execution gas, and the number of tokens per transaction. If your requirements exceed these limits, you may need to [contact the Chainlink Labs Team](https://chain.link/ccip-contact).

    Following these best practices ensures that your contract is robust, future-proof, and compliant with CCIP standards.
  </Aside>
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Handling fees and token approvals" number={4} startOpen={false}>
  `_handleFeeAndTokenApprovals` validates balances and grants the router the required token approvals before sending. It handles three scenarios:

  1. **Native fee** (`_feeTokenAddress == address(0)`): Validates that `msg.value` covers the CCIP fee. Checks the contract's token balance and approves the router to transfer the token amount.
  2. **Same token for fee and transfer** (`_token == _feeTokenAddress`): Checks the contract holds enough to cover fee + transfer amount combined. Approves the router for the total.
  3. **Different ERC-20 tokens**: Checks and approves the fee token and the transfer token separately.

  ```solidity filename="_handleFeeAndTokenApprovals"
    function _handleFeeAndTokenApprovals(
        IRouterClient _router,
        address _token,
        uint256 _amount,
        address _feeTokenAddress,
        uint256 _ccipFee
    ) private {
        if (_feeTokenAddress == address(0)) {
            if (msg.value < _ccipFee) {
                revert InsufficientNativeForFees(msg.value, _ccipFee);
            }
            uint256 tokenBalance = IERC20(_token).balanceOf(address(this));
            if (_amount > tokenBalance) {
                revert NotEnoughBalance(tokenBalance, _amount);
            }
            IERC20(_token).approve(address(_router), _amount);
        } else if (_token == _feeTokenAddress) {
            uint256 totalAmount = _ccipFee + _amount;
            uint256 tokenBalance = IERC20(_token).balanceOf(address(this));
            if (totalAmount > tokenBalance) {
                revert NotEnoughBalance(tokenBalance, totalAmount);
            }
            IERC20(_token).approve(address(_router), totalAmount);
        } else {
            uint256 feeTokenBalance = IERC20(_feeTokenAddress).balanceOf(address(this));
            if (_ccipFee > feeTokenBalance) {
                revert NotEnoughBalance(feeTokenBalance, _ccipFee);
            }
            uint256 tokenBalance = IERC20(_token).balanceOf(address(this));
            if (_amount > tokenBalance) {
                revert NotEnoughBalance(tokenBalance, _amount);
            }
            IERC20(_feeTokenAddress).approve(address(_router), _ccipFee);
            IERC20(_token).approve(address(_router), _amount);
        }
    }
  ```
</Accordion>

<GitHubCard title="ProgrammableTokenTransfers.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/contracts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/ProgrammableTokenTransfers.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
  Check out the complete contract code on Github.
</GitHubCard>

## Tutorial

Let's get started! Choose your preferred development environment below.

### Foundry

Best for **Solidity-native** workflows that prefer a modular, powerful scripting framework.

<Aside type="note" title="Want to set up a dev environment from scratch? ">
  Check out the [Getting started page](/ccip/evm/getting-started#foundry) for detailed instructions on setting up a
  Foundry dev environment from scratch.
</Aside>

<Accordion title="Bootstrap a new Foundry project" number={1} startOpen={false}>
  <GitHubCard title="Foundry Starter Kit" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Clone the Foundry Starter Kit for a smoother setup.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Clone the [CCIP 2.0 template](https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip) repository, and open a terminal inside the project directory:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  git clone https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip.git && cd docs-ccip
  ```

  2. If you don't already have a Foundry keystore, use the `cast` command to create a new one. Here, `PRIVATE_KEY` is the alias you assign to this keystore entry -- Foundry will prompt you to enter the actual private key and a password to encrypt it:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  cast wallet import --interactive PRIVATE_KEY
  ```

  And use the `cast wallet list` command to verify:

  <ClickToZoom src="/images/ccip/tutorials/ccip-getting-started-evm-2.png" alt="Foundry keystore list command output" />

  <Aside type="caution" title="Note">
    As of writing, Foundry supports keystore encryption only for private keys (not generic strings), so in this tutorial
    we only encrypt the `PRIVATE_KEY`.
  </Aside>

  3. Install dependencies:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  pnpm install --no-lockfile
  ```

  4. Configure your RPC endpoints in a `.env` file:

  ```env filename=".env"
  AVALANCHE_FUJI_RPC_URL=https://your-rpc-provider/api-key
  ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://your-rpc-provider/api-key
  ```

  5. Load the environment variables:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  source .env
  ```

  <Aside type="caution" title="Run this in every new terminal session">
    If you open a new terminal, you must run `source .env` again. To verify, run `echo $ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL` and
    confirm it prints your URL.
  </Aside>

  6. Run the following command to compile all the contracts:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  forge build
  ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Configure allowlists" number={3} startOpen={false}>
  As a best practice, allowlist specific contracts to prevent unauthorized message delivery between your sender and receiver.

  <GitHubCard title="Configure.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/Configure.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Export the addresses of the previously deployed contracts so that they're available in the terminal:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  export ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_CONTRACT=<Sender Contract address> && export AVALANCHE_FUJI_CONTRACT=<Receiver Contract Address>
  ```

  2. The `Configure.s.sol` script handles both sides in a single command -- it allowlists the destination chain on the sender contract and allowlists the chain-sender pair on the receiver contract:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI forge script foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/Configure.s.sol --account PRIVATE_KEY --broadcast -vv
  ```

  3. **Optional -- enable bidirectional messaging:** If you want the destination contract to also send messages back to the source, run the reversed command:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI DEST_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA forge script foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/Configure.s.sol --account PRIVATE_KEY --broadcast -vv
  ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Transfer tokens + data and pay in native gas" number={5} startOpen={false}>
  You will transfer *0.001 CCIP-BnM* and a text. The CCIP fees for using CCIP will be paid in Ethereum Sepolia's native `ETH`. The `SendMessagePayNative.s.sol` script does the following:

  - Fund the sender contract with CCIP-BnM tokens.
  - Send a CCIP message, passing the native fee directly via `{value: ccipFee}`.

  #### How to set your own Finality?

  CCIP 2.0 allows you to make your own tradeoffs between speed and security.
  You can do this by editing the `BLOCK_CONFIRMATIONS` constant in the script. Set it to `0` for default finality, or a value above the pool's minimum for custom finality.

  ```solidity filename="SendMessagePayNative.s.sol"
  // Imports ........

  contract SendMessagePayNative is Script, BlockConfirmationHelper {

      HelperConfig public helperConfig;
      // Update this single value to see how CCIP behaves with different finality settings.
      // 0 = "use default finality"
      uint16 internal constant BLOCK_CONFIRMATIONS = 20;

      function run() external {
      // Rest of the code...
      }
  }
  ```

  To run the script:

  1. Run the script using the following command:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI forge script foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/SendMessagePayNative.s.sol --account PRIVATE_KEY --broadcast -vv
  ```

  The script will output the message ID and the CCIP explorer URL.

  Your terminal should look like this:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  == Logs ==

    ========================================
    📡 CCIP Message Transfer - Pay with Native
    ========================================
    Source Chain: Ethereum Sepolia
    Destination Chain: Avalanche Fuji
    Sender: 0x087413C920BF11a3b10C12e60D6a8d3370b392F6
    Receiver: 0x132e3f530f838147Bf32DB11AD5b18Ee036a6Ac6
    ========================================


  [Pre-validation] Checking block confirmations...
    Requested block confirmations: 20
    Minimum required block confirmations: 1
    ✅ Custom finality validated

  [Step 1] Funding contract with CCIP-BnM...
    ✅ Contract funded with CCIP-BnM


  [Step 2] Sending CCIP message with native token fee ( ETH )...
    Required CCIP fee (in WEI): 630000000000000

    ========================================
    ✅ Message sent successfully!
    ========================================
    CCIP messageId: 0xa62f75cb11c73716d0338121960dd44ec39747270c1ed8395683bff3e8331f36
    CCIP Explorer:
    https://ccip-ui-ccip-20-holding-branch-chainlinklabs.vercel.app/#/side-drawer/msg/0xa62f75cb11c73716d0338121960dd44ec39747270c1ed8395683bff3e8331f36
  ```

  Check out the complete file on Github here:

  <GitHubCard title="SendMessagePayNative.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/SendMessagePayNative.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Verify receipt on the destination chain" number={7} startOpen={false}>
  The `GetLastReceivedMessageDetails.s.sol` script queries the receiver contract for the last received message. This is a read-only operation: no transaction is broadcasted.

  1. Run the script, passing the `CHAIN` where you deployed the receiver:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI forge script foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/GetLastReceivedMessageDetails.s.sol
  ```

  Your terminal should look something like this:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  == Logs ==

    ========================================
    🔍 Verify Received Message
    ========================================
    Chain: Avalanche Fuji
    Receiver Address: 0x132e3f530f838147Bf32DB11AD5b18Ee036a6Ac6
    ========================================

    Checking for received message...

    ❌ No message received yet.
    Please wait a bit longer and try again.
  ```

  Check out the complete file on Github here:

  <GitHubCard title="GetLastReceivedMessageDetails.s.sol" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/foundry/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/GetLastReceivedMessageDetails.s.sol" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>
</Accordion>

### Hardhat

Best for devs looking for a mature, Typescript-based smart contract development framework.

<Aside type="note" title="Want to set up a dev environment from scratch? ">
  Check out the [Getting started page](/ccip/evm/getting-started#hardhat-3) for detailed instructions on setting up a
  Hardhat dev environment from scratch.
</Aside>

<Accordion title="Bootstrap a new Hardhat project" number={1} startOpen={false}>
  <GitHubCard title="CCIP Starter Kit" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Clone the starter kit (contains both Hardhat and Foundry code) for a smoother setup.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Clone the [CCIP 2.0 template](https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip) repository, and open a terminal inside the project directory:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  git clone https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip.git && cd docs-ccip
  ```

  2. Store your private key and RPC endpoints in the Hardhat keystore. Hardhat 3's `configVariable()` resolves secrets from the encrypted keystore, so no `.env` file is needed:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  npx hardhat keystore set PRIVATE_KEY
  npx hardhat keystore set ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL
  npx hardhat keystore set AVALANCHE_FUJI_RPC_URL
  ```

  3. Install dependencies:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  pnpm install --no-lockfile
  ```

  4. Compile the contracts:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  npx hardhat compile
  ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Configure allowlists" number={3} startOpen={false}>
  As a best practice, allowlist specific contracts to prevent unauthorized message delivery between your sender and receiver.

  <GitHubCard title="configure.ts" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/configure.ts" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>

  1. Export the addresses of the previously deployed contracts so that they're available in the terminal:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  export ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA_CONTRACT=<Sender Contract address> && export AVALANCHE_FUJI_CONTRACT=<Receiver Contract Address>
  ```

  2. The `configure.ts` script handles both sides in a single command -- it allowlists the destination chain on the sender contract and allowlists the chain-sender pair on the receiver contract:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI npx hardhat run hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/configure.ts
  ```

  3. **Optional -- enable bidirectional messaging:** If you want the destination contract to also send messages back to the source, run the reversed command:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI DEST_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA npx hardhat run hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/configure/configure.ts
  ```
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Transfer tokens + data and pay in native gas" number={5} startOpen={false}>
  You will transfer *0.001 CCIP-BnM* and a text. The CCIP fees for using CCIP will be paid in Ethereum Sepolia's native `ETH`. The `send-message-pay-native.ts` script does the following:

  - Fund the sender contract with CCIP-BnM tokens.
  - Send a CCIP message, passing the native fee directly via `value: ccipFee`.

  #### How to set your own Finality?

  CCIP 2.0 allows you to make your own tradeoffs between speed and security.
  You can do this by editing the `BLOCK_CONFIRMATIONS` constant in the script. Set it to `0` for default finality, or a value above the pool's minimum for custom finality.

  ```typescript filename="send-message-pay-native.ts"
  const MESSAGE = "Hello World From Hardhat Script for CCIP 2.0 (Native Payment)!"
  const CCIP_BNM_AMOUNT = parseUnits("0.001", 18)
  const GAS_LIMIT = 200_000
  const BLOCK_CONFIRMATIONS = 20
  ```

  To run the script:

  1. Run the script using the following command:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  SOURCE_CHAIN=ETHEREUM_SEPOLIA DEST_CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI npx hardhat run hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/send-message-pay-native.ts
  ```

  The script will output the message ID and the CCIP explorer URL.

  Your terminal should look like this:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  ========================================
  📡 CCIP Message Transfer - Pay with Native
  ========================================
  Source Chain: Ethereum Sepolia
  Destination Chain: Avalanche Fuji
  Sender: 0xda5a301e9a0f54dee8ebec036e3accae13b38c9c
  Receiver: 0x3550780899f85379bedc607ab0ddfb7c4b64d789
  ========================================

  [hardhat-keystore] Enter the password: *********

  [Pre-validation] Checking block confirmations...
  Requested block confirmations: 20
  Minimum required block confirmations: 1
  ✅ Custom finality validated

  [Step 1] Funding contract with CCIP-BnM...
  ✅ Contract funded with CCIP-BnM

  [Step 2] Sending CCIP message with native token fee ( ETH )...
  Required CCIP fee (in WEI): 630000000000000

  ========================================
  ✅ Message sent successfully!
  ========================================
  CCIP messageId: 0x6f14ffe8fbb0d5d4c95306fe7c3da9f284388b55f8e2f5d366af76baa4309b23
  CCIP Explorer:
  https://ccip-ui-ccip-20-holding-branch-chainlinklabs.vercel.app/#/side-drawer/msg/0x6f14ffe8fbb0d5d4c95306fe7c3da9f284388b55f8e2f5d366af76baa4309b23
  ```

  Check out the complete file on Github here:

  <GitHubCard title="send-message-pay-native.ts" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/send-message-pay-native.ts" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>
</Accordion>

<Accordion title="Verify receipt on the destination chain" number={7} startOpen={false}>
  The `get-last-received-message-details.ts` script queries the receiver contract for the last received message. This is a read-only operation: no transaction is actually broadcasted to the destination chain.

  1. Run the script, passing the `CHAIN` where you deployed the receiver:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  CHAIN=AVALANCHE_FUJI npx hardhat run hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/get-last-received-message-details.ts
  ```

  Your terminal should look something like this:

  ```bash filename="Terminal"
  ========================================
  🔍 Verify Received Message
  ========================================
  Chain: Avalanche Fuji
  Receiver Address: 0x3550780899f85379bedc607ab0ddfb7c4b64d789
  ========================================

  Checking for received message...

  ❌ No message received yet.
  Please wait a bit longer and try again.
  ```

  Check out the complete file on Github here:

  <GitHubCard title="get-last-received-message-details.ts" href="https://github.com/smartcontractkit/docs-ccip/blob/main/hardhat/scripts/tutorials/programmable-token-transfers/interact/get-last-received-message-details.ts" iconName="github-svg-card">
    Check out the complete script code on Github.
  </GitHubCard>
</Accordion>

