# Overview
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/product-uis/overview

Product UIs are interactive tools for configuring, operating, and inspecting CCIP without writing code.

## Configure and Operate

Use these tools when you want to actively manage tokens or move assets across chains.

### Token Manager

Configure and manage cross-chain tokens using a no-code interface.

Use Token Manager to:

- Enable tokens for cross-chain use
- Configure token pools and permissions
- Manage rate limits and supported networks

→ [View Token Manager](/ccip/product-uis/token-manager)

### Transporter

Send tokens across chains using a simple UI.

Use Transporter to:

- Transfer supported tokens between chains
- Test cross-chain transfers without writing code

→ [View Transporter](/ccip/product-uis/transporter)

## Inspect and Validate

Use these tools when you want to understand, debug, or verify cross-chain activity.

### Directory

Discover supported networks, tokens, and CCIP capabilities.

Use Directory to:

- Check network and token support
- View available routes and configurations
- Validate setup options before deploying

→ [View Directory](/ccip/product-uis/directory)

### Explorer

Inspect and verify cross-chain messages and token transfers.

Use Explorer to:

- Track message delivery and execution
- Debug failed or pending transfers
- Inspect cross-chain activity

→ [View Explorer](/ccip/product-uis/explorer)

---

# Token Manager
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/product-uis/token-manager

Token Manager is a **no-code interface** for enabling and managing cross-chain tokens using Chainlink CCIP.

It is designed for asset issuers and protocol owners who want to configure cross-chain token behavior with built-in controls and safeguards, without writing smart contracts.

## When to use Token Manager

Use Token Manager if you:

- Issue or control a token contract
- Want to enable a token for cross-chain use without writing code
- Need configurable controls such as rate limits and permissions
- Prefer a guided, UI-driven configuration experience

## When not to use Token Manager

Token Manager may not be the right choice if:

- You do not control the token contract
- You only need to transfer tokens as part of an application workflow
- You require custom automation or advanced configuration logic

In those cases, see **Build a cross-chain app** or follow the code-based issuer tutorials instead.

## What you can do

With Token Manager, you can:

- Enable tokens for cross-chain use
- Configure token pool types and transfer behavior
- Set rate limits and permissions
- Add or manage supported networks over time
- Monitor and update existing token configurations

## How Token Manager fits into CCIP

Token Manager is an **execution surface**, not a tutorial.

It complements:

- **Use Cases**, which help you decide what to build
- **Tutorials**, which provide step-by-step workflows
- **Developer Tools**, which document APIs and interfaces

Use Token Manager when you’re ready to act, not when you’re still evaluating options.

## Get started

→ [Open Token Manager](https://tokenmanager.chain.link/)

---

# Transporter
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/product-uis/transporter

Transporter is a **no-code interface** for sending tokens across blockchains using Chainlink CCIP.

It is designed for developers, operators, and users who want to move supported tokens between chains without writing or deploying smart contracts.

## When to use Transporter

Use Transporter if you:

- Want to send supported tokens across chains quickly
- Need a simple way to test or demonstrate cross-chain transfers
- Prefer a UI-driven experience over writing code
- Want to move tokens without building an application

## When not to use Transporter

Transporter may not be the right choice if:

- You need to build cross-chain logic into an application
- You want to send messages or tokens with custom data
- You require automated or programmatic transfers

In those cases, see **Build a cross-chain app** or follow the application developer tutorials instead.

## What you can do

With Transporter, you can:

- Transfer supported tokens between chains
- Select source and destination networks
- Monitor the status of cross-chain transfers
- Validate CCIP token transfer behavior without code

## How Transporter fits into CCIP

Transporter is an **execution surface**, not a tutorial.

It complements:

- **Use Cases**, which help you decide what to build
- **Tutorials**, which show how to implement workflows in code
- **Developer Tools**, which provide programmatic interfaces

Use Transporter when you want to move tokens directly, not when you’re building custom application logic.

## Get started

→ [Open Transporter](https://www.transporter.io/)

---

# Directory
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/product-uis/directory

Directory is a **read-only interface** for discovering CCIP-supported networks, tokens, and capabilities.

It is designed for developers, operators, and institutions who want to understand what is supported before building, configuring, or executing cross-chain workflows.

## When to use Directory

Use Directory if you:

- Want to check which networks are supported by CCIP
- Need to see which tokens are enabled for cross-chain use
- Are validating routes, capabilities, or configuration options
- Want a high-level view of CCIP availability across chains

## When not to use Directory

Directory may not be the right choice if:

- You want to configure or manage tokens
- You need to send tokens across chains
- You want to inspect execution status or message details

In those cases, use **Token Manager**, **Transporter**, or **Explorer** instead.

## What you can do

With Directory, you can:

- View supported source and destination networks
- Discover tokens enabled for cross-chain transfers
- Check CCIP feature availability by network
- Validate options before configuring or deploying

## How Directory fits into CCIP

Directory is a **discovery surface**, not an execution or configuration tool.

It complements:

- **Use Cases**, which help you decide what to build
- **Product UIs**, which let you configure or execute actions
- **Tutorials**, which show how workflows are implemented
- **Developer Tools**, which provide programmatic interfaces

Use Directory when you need clarity on what’s supported, not when you’re ready to act.

## Get started

→ [Open Directory](https://docs.chain.link/ccip/directory/mainnet)

---

# Explorer
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/product-uis/explorer

Explorer is a **read-only interface** for inspecting and verifying cross-chain messages and token transfers executed via Chainlink CCIP.

It is designed for developers, operators, and institutions who need visibility into cross-chain activity, delivery status, and execution outcomes.

## When to use Explorer

Use Explorer if you:

- Want to inspect cross-chain messages or token transfers
- Need to verify delivery and execution status
- Are debugging failed, pending, or delayed transfers
- Need an auditable view of cross-chain activity

## When not to use Explorer

Explorer may not be the right choice if:

- You want to configure or execute cross-chain actions
- You need to send tokens or messages
- You want to manage token settings or permissions

In those cases, use **Token Manager**, **Transporter**, or follow the relevant tutorials instead.

## What you can do

With Explorer, you can:

- Search for cross-chain messages and transfers
- View execution status and timestamps
- Inspect source and destination chain details
- Verify CCIP message identifiers and outcomes

## How Explorer fits into CCIP

Explorer is an **observability surface**, not an execution tool.

It complements:

- **Product UIs**, which are used to configure or execute actions
- **Tutorials**, which explain how workflows are built
- **Developer Tools**, which provide programmatic access to CCIP data

Use Explorer when you need visibility and verification, not when you want to take action.

## Get started

→ [Open Explorer](https://ccip.chain.link/)

