# CCIP Architecture - Key Concepts
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/key-concepts
Last Updated: 2025-05-19

This page explains the high-level, chain-agnostic concepts behind Chainlink CCIP. We recommend reading these concepts before diving deep into the architecture pages.

## Prerequisites

Before diving into Chainlink CCIP, ensure you understand these fundamental blockchain concepts:

- **Accounts / Addresses**: Many blockchains distinguish between user-controlled addresses (called Externally Owned Accounts (EOAs) on EVM blockchains) and onchain program addresses (often called contract accounts in EVM ecosystems). Even if the name or the underlying account model differs, the principle is that a user's private key or authority controls one address (EOA) while the other is controlled by onchain code or logic (contract account).

- **Smart Contracts**: On EVM-based blockchains, these are pieces of logic (often written in languages like Solidity) that get compiled into bytecode and deployed onchain. Other blockchains—such as SVM-based (e.g., Solana), **Aptos**, or **Sui**—may refer to these as "modules," "programs," or simply "onchain code." The basic idea is the same: executable code that lives on the blockchain and enforces rules without centralized control.

- **Decentralized Applications (dApps)**: [dApps](https://ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/dapps/) are applications that use onchain logic to manage data and transactions.

- **Token Standards**: Many chains use standards (like [ERC-20](https://ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/standards/tokens/erc-20/) on EVM-based blockchains) or equivalents (e.g., [SPL tokens on Solana](https://spl.solana.com/token), the [Fungible Asset standard on Aptos](https://aptos.dev/en/build/smart-contracts/fungible-asset), or the [Coin standard on Sui](https://docs.sui.io/standards/coin)) to represent fungible tokens.

- **Merkle Trees**: A data structure that summarizes and verifies large information sets with small cryptographic proofs. To learn more, try this [tutorial](https://ethereum.org/en/developers/tutorials/merkle-proofs-for-offline-data-integrity/).

## Blockchain Families

When you see references to "smart contracts" or "onchain programs," they are executed within a virtual machine (VM). Different blockchains implement different VMs with unique design choices:

- **EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine)**: Used by Ethereum and many other EVM-compatible chains. Contracts are typically written in languages like [Solidity](https://docs.soliditylang.org/) or [Vyper](https://vyper.readthedocs.io/), compiled to EVM bytecode, and executed in a single-threaded environment. Read this [page](https://ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/evm/) to learn more.

- **SVM (Solana Virtual Machine)**: Solana uses the Sealevel parallelization engine to execute onchain programs compiled to Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) bytecode. This parallel processing approach can handle multiple simultaneous transactions. Read this [page](https://solana.com/news/sealevel---parallel-processing-thousands-of-smart-contracts) to learn more.

- **Aptos**: The Aptos blockchain uses the Move language and its own implementation of the Move Virtual Machine (MoveVM). The MoveVM is designed for safety and verification, and Aptos processes transactions in parallel using its Block-STM execution engine. Read this [page](https://aptos.dev/en/network/blockchain/move) to learn more.

- **Sui**: The Sui blockchain uses a variant of the Move language. Its architecture is object-centric and designed for high-throughput, parallel execution of transactions, enabling horizontal scaling. Read this [page](https://docs.sui.io/concepts/sui-move-concepts) to learn more.

## Cross-Chain dApps

**Cross-chain dApps** are decentralized applications designed to run on—or interact with—multiple blockchains. By leveraging CCIP for cross-chain interoperability, these dApps can:

- Execute transactions, send data, and transfer tokens between different blockchains.
- Provide their users with access to features or liquidity across multiple ecosystems via a unified experience.
- Specialize in the strengths of each underlying chain, such as high throughput, low fees, or specialized contract (or module) functionality.

Because of this multi-chain support, cross-chain dApps can offer broader functionality than dApps confined to a single blockchain.

## Blockchain Finality

**Blockchain finality** is the assurance that past transactions included on a given blockchain are extremely difficult or impossible to revert. Finality varies across different blockchains. Some blockchains offer instant finality, while others require multiple block confirmations.

Finality is crucial for cross-chain transfers, both for arbitrary messaging and non-intents-based token transfers. It ensures that funds are released/minted or that any specified action is executed on the destination chain only after the source chain action (such as a lock or burn) is finalized. This minimizes loss of funds (in the case of token transfers) or unintended actions (in the case of arbitrary messaging) due to source chain reorganizations.

To learn more about finality and how CCIP handles this, read the [CCIP Execution Latency](/ccip/v1/ccip-execution-latency) conceptual guide.

## Lane

A **lane** in CCIP is a conceptual unidirectional path from one specific blockchain (the source) to another (the destination). For example, the lane from Blockchain A to Blockchain B is distinct from the lane from Blockchain B to Blockchain A. This concept helps in configuring settings that apply uniquely to each path, such as rate limits or other parameters. For instance, sending a message from an EVM blockchain to an SVM blockchain may require different parameters compared to sending a message in the reverse direction.

## Node Operators

Organizations with specialized expertise in running Chainlink nodes. Each operator can run multiple nodes across different decentralized oracle networks.

## Decentralized Oracle Network (DON)

A **decentralized oracle network (DON)** consists of multiple independent Chainlink nodes. These nodes monitor and retrieve data from specified sources (e.g., a source chain), reach consensus off-chain, and post results to a blockchain (e.g., a destination chain) in a [trust-minimized manner](https://blog.chain.link/what-is-trust-minimization/). For more details on the DONs used in CCIP, refer to the CCIP Offchain architecture page.

---

# Offchain Architecture
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/offchain/overview

This page describes the **offchain responsibilities and coordination model** of Chainlink CCIP. It explains how decentralized oracle networks (DONs) observe cross-chain activity, reach consensus, and provide verified inputs that enable deterministic onchain execution.

This page focuses on **roles, trust boundaries, and verification flow**, not on protocol configuration, node operation, or OCR internals. Definitions of CCIP primitives (such as DONs, commit reports, execution reports, and the Risk Management Network) are covered in [Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/v1/concepts/core-concepts).

## Role of the offchain layer

The offchain layer is responsible for **observation, verification, and coordination** across blockchains. It enables CCIP to:

- Verify cross-chain state without relying on a single chain
- Reach decentralized consensus on message validity and ordering
- Coordinate efficient execution on destination chains

The offchain layer does **not** custody tokens or execute application logic. Instead, it produces cryptographically verifiable reports that the onchain layer enforces.

## Decentralized oracle networks (DONs)

CCIP’s offchain architecture is operated by Chainlink’s decentralized oracle networks using the Offchain Reporting (OCR) protocol.

In the current architecture, CCIP uses a single **Role DON**, in which participating nodes may take on different responsibilities depending on the workflow stage. These responsibilities are implemented through distinct OCR plugins.

### Commit role

Nodes operating in the commit role are responsible for:

- Observing relevant events on supported source chains
- Validating message data and associated metadata
- Reaching decentralized consensus on message ordering
- Producing commit reports that summarize verified cross-chain state

Commit reports may include:

- Merkle roots representing batches of verified messages
- Price updates required for accurate fee calculation

Once consensus is reached, commit reports are submitted onchain for enforcement.

### Execution role

Nodes operating in the execution role are responsible for:

- Monitoring destination chains for committed but unexecuted messages
- Verifying that execution conditions are satisfied
- Coordinating efficient batching and execution strategies
- Submitting execution reports for onchain verification

Execution coordination considers chain-specific constraints such as gas limits and execution costs, while preserving protocol correctness.

> Although these responsibilities are often described as “Committing” and “Executing” DONs, they are not separate networks. They represent role-based behavior within the same underlying Role DON.

## Conceptual offchain workflow

At a high level, CCIP’s offchain workflow proceeds as follows:

1. Offchain nodes observe cross-chain events emitted by CCIP contracts on source chains.
2. Nodes independently validate observations and reach decentralized consensus.
3. Verified state is aggregated into cryptographic reports.
4. Reports are submitted onchain, where they are enforced by CCIP smart contracts.

This separation ensures that cross-chain verification remains decentralized, while execution remains deterministic and auditable.

## Risk management and safety controls

CCIP incorporates additional offchain and hybrid mechanisms to manage risk and respond to exceptional conditions.

The **Risk Management Network (RMN)** enables CCIP to:

- Detect and respond to anomalous cross-chain behavior
- Temporarily block message processing from affected chains
- Provide an additional safety layer beyond standard consensus

RMN decisions are enforced onchain through explicit checks, ensuring that safety controls are transparent and verifiable.

> The RMN architecture continues to evolve to support more modular security and compliance models. Current enforcement mechanisms are described in the Onchain Architecture documentation.

## Trust boundaries and guarantees

The offchain layer provides the following guarantees to the onchain layer:

- Cross-chain observations are validated by multiple independent nodes
- No single node can unilaterally approve or execute a message
- All offchain decisions are materialized as verifiable onchain inputs

Conversely, the offchain layer relies on the onchain layer to:

- Enforce execution correctness
- Custody and move tokens
- Record final execution outcomes

This division of responsibility is central to CCIP’s security model.

## Learn more

How verified reports are enforced onchain

- [Architecture Overview](/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/overview): End-to-end system flow and boundaries
- [Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/v1/concepts/core-concepts): Definitions of DONs, reports, and verification models

---

# Onchain Architecture
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/onchain
Last Updated: 2025-09-03

This section details the onchain components of the CCIP architecture, including routers, onramps, offramps, commit stores, token pools, and receiver contracts or programs. These components operate directly on blockchains to enable secure cross-chain communication. The implementation varies by blockchain family (EVM, Solana, Aptos), but the core architecture remains consistent across all supported chains.

---

# Onchain Architecture
Source: https://docs.chain.link/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/onchain/overview

This page describes the **onchain responsibilities and execution guarantees** of Chainlink CCIP. It explains how CCIP smart contracts on source and destination chains enforce message delivery, token custody, and execution correctness, based on verified inputs from the offchain layer.

This page focuses on **system-level behavior**, not on application integration, configuration steps, or API usage. Definitions of CCIP primitives (such as the Router, OnRamp, OffRamp, Token Pools, and RMN contracts) are covered in [Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/v1/concepts/core-concepts).

## Role of the onchain layer

The onchain layer is responsible for **deterministic enforcement** of cross-chain actions. While CCIP’s offchain components observe, verify, and reach consensus on cross-chain state, the onchain layer is where:

- Tokens are locked, burned, minted, or released
- Execution guarantees are enforced
- Messages are routed to receivers
- Final execution outcomes are recorded onchain

All application-level effects of a CCIP message occur onchain.

## Core onchain responsibilities

At a high level, CCIP’s onchain contracts are responsible for the following functions:

### Unified entry and routing

Each supported blockchain exposes a single CCIP entry point through which applications and EOAs interact with the protocol. This entry point:

- Accepts outbound cross-chain messages on the source chain
- Routes verified inbound messages to receivers on the destination chain
- Acts as the stable interface between applications and CCIP’s internal contracts

### Message validation and enforcement

Onchain contracts enforce protocol-level constraints, including:

- Message structure validation
- Chain allowlists and curse checks
- Execution ordering guarantees (where applicable)
- Verification of offchain consensus outputs

Messages that fail validation or violate protocol constraints cannot be executed.

### Token custody and accounting

When a CCIP message includes token transfers, onchain contracts ensure that:

- Tokens are securely locked or burned on the source chain
- Corresponding tokens are released or minted on the destination chain
- Token pool–specific rate limits and accounting rules are enforced

Token handling logic is executed exclusively onchain.

### Execution finality and observability

The onchain layer records the final execution state of each message, ensuring that:

- Successful and failed executions are unambiguously recorded
- Execution outcomes are publicly observable
- Failed executions can be retried or manually executed where permitted

## Source-chain behavior (conceptual)

On the source chain, CCIP’s onchain contracts coordinate the initiation of a cross-chain message:

- A sender submits a message through the CCIP onchain interface
- Fees are validated and accounted for
- Token transfers (if any) are processed via the appropriate token pools
- A unique message identifier is generated
- An onchain event is emitted for offchain observation

At this point, the message becomes eligible for offchain verification and consensus.

## Destination-chain behavior (conceptual)

On the destination chain, CCIP’s onchain contracts enforce execution based on verified reports:

- Verified commit reports are accepted onchain
- Merkle proofs are checked against committed roots
- Curse status and protocol constraints are revalidated
- Token transfers (if any) are finalized
- Application payloads are delivered to the designated receiver

The message’s final execution state is recorded onchain as either successful or failed.

## Failure handling and manual execution

If a message fails execution (for example, due to insufficient gas or receiver logic errors), CCIP supports **permissionless manual execution** under defined conditions. This mechanism ensures that messages are not permanently stuck due to transient failures, while preserving protocol safety.

Manual execution details are covered in **Tutorials → Advanced → Manual Execution**.

## Upgradability and configuration model

CCIP’s onchain architecture is designed to evolve while preserving stability and predictability for developers.

### Immutable entry points

Certain contracts, such as the CCIP Router, are intentionally immutable. This ensures a stable and reliable interface for applications across protocol upgrades.

### Configurable and replaceable components

Other onchain components may be updated through one of two mechanisms:

- **Onchain configuration changes**, applied through governed setter functions
- **Contract redeployment**, where new contract instances are deployed and referenced by existing components

This approach allows CCIP to introduce new features, improve performance, or respond to security considerations without disrupting application integrations.

### Governance and safety controls

All onchain changes are gated by a role-based timelock and multisignature governance process. This ensures:

- Transparent proposal review
- Node operator oversight and veto rights
- Public verifiability of pending and executed changes

## Learn more

- [Offchain Architecture](/ccip/v1/concepts/architecture/offchain/overview): How DONs verify, commit, and coordinate execution
- [Core CCIP Concepts](/ccip/v1/concepts/core-concepts): Definitions of CCIP primitives and models
- [Tutorials](/ccip/v1/tutorials/overview): How to send messages and transfer tokens using CCIP
- [API Reference](/ccip/v1/api-reference/evm): Contract interfaces and revert reasons

